  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch05.s06",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch05.s06.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r01",
            "text": "How can you tell whether a scratch-built GPT is really working after pretrained weights are assigned, and what becomes possible once it does?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r02",
            "text": "A simple check is to call the \"generate\" function with the loaded model, using input token IDs for the prompt \"every effort moves you\", a maximum of 25 new tokens, temperature 1.5, and top-k 50.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2607",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2610",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2612",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2615",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2617",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2620",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2622",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2625",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2628"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r03",
            "text": "With the GPT-2 weights loaded, generation does not take much time because the model already has pretrained weights.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2659",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2661",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2664",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2666",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2667"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r04",
            "text": "The generated continuation is much more coherent than the earlier output produced without GPT-2 weights.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2643",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2646",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2648",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2650",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2683",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2686"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r05",
            "text": "The weights are treated as loaded correctly because the model produces coherent text and at least the words make sense.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2899",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2901",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2903",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2931",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2933",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2936",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2937",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2939"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p01.r06",
            "text": "Even a tiny mistake in the loading process would cause the model to fail.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2941",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2943"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s06.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p02.01",
            "text": "Once the model generates sensible text, you can start probing how its sampling behavior changes.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p02.02",
            "text": "Increasing the temperature makes the output more random because increasing the temperature increases the entropy.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2747",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2749",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2751",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2753"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p02.03",
            "text": "For example, setting the temperature to 10 is used specifically to see the effect of a much higher temperature value.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2728",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2730",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2733",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2742",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2744",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2745"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p02.04",
            "text": "At that setting, the text is expected to become a bit random, and the resulting output is described as random.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2747",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2749",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2763",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2764"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p02.05",
            "text": "Having your own GPT implementation opens the door to experimentation, creativity, hyperparameter tuning, and research that is not available in the same way through ChatGPT.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2735",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2737",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2739",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2767",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2769",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2771",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2774"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch05.s06.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r01",
            "text": "That freedom also extends to the model itself, not just to sampling settings.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r02",
            "text": "To change the architecture, you go to the defined GPT model and add or subtract layers.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2776",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2777",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2781",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2783",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2789",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2791",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2794",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2796"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r03",
            "text": "One concrete change is to reduce the number of transformer blocks if you want to test a smaller language model instead of using 12 transformer blocks.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2796",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2798",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2800",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2802"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r04",
            "text": "Because the implementation works on a local computer and runs fast, you can iterate and test many choices.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2810",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2812",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2814",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2816",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2818"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r05",
            "text": "The parameters named for experimentation include top-k, maximum new tokens, the number of attention heads, the number of transformer blocks, and the optimizer step size.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2818",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2820",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2822",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2824",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2826",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2828",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2829"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch05.s06.p03.r06",
            "text": "The optimizer used here is Adam with a learning rate of 5 into 10 to the minus 4 and weight decay 0.1, and those settings can also be changed to check their effect on the output.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2832",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2835",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2839",
              "yXrGeDNuymY@2842"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
